// contrib/http2/server.mere — an h2c connection, enough of one to serve gRPC.
//
// TLS IS NOT MISSING BY OVERSIGHT. gRPC over cleartext HTTP/2 ("h2c", prior
// knowledge) is what `grpcurl -plaintext` and every gRPC client's insecure mode
// speak: the connection opens with 24 literal bytes and no negotiation. Measured,
// not assumed — a capture of grpcurl's first packet is exactly the preface
// followed by an empty SETTINGS frame. So a server can be useful before anything
// here knows what ALPN is.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, stated up front because a reader will look for it:
//
//   * ONE CONNECTION AT A TIME. `tcp_accept` returns, the connection is served to
//     completion, and then the next one is taken. mhttpd already showed how to
//     spawn a handler per connection with a buffer pool; doing it here as well
//     would mix two problems in one file's first version.
//   * ONE STREAM'S STATE AT A TIME. A client that interleaves two requests on one
//     connection before finishing either would be mis-served. Unary gRPC does not,
//     and the parity harness checks the case that exists rather than pretending
//     about the case that does not.
//   * NO INTERLEAVED BIDIRECTIONAL STREAMING. A handler is called after the request
//     half-closes, so a response cannot begin before the request ends. Many messages
//     in each direction work (see FLOW CONTROL AND STREAMING below); a response that
//     must react to a request message *while more are arriving* does not.
//
// FLOW CONTROL AND STREAMING, and the order the limits actually bite in. All four
// numbers below were measured against a python `h2` client, not read off the RFC:
//
//   1. `wbuf` HOLDS 16384 BYTES AND NOTHING CHECKED IT. `mem_copy_bytes` is a
//      `memcpy` into a bump-allocated arena that records no allocation sizes, so a
//      larger response wrote past the buffer, clobbered the next allocation, and
//      RETURNED THE LENGTH AS IF IT HAD SUCCEEDED. Demonstrated with a sentinel: 32
//      bytes into a 16-byte allocation changed the byte after it and reported 32.
//      `send` now takes the capacity and fails loudly. A bound nothing enforces is
//      not a bound.
//   2. SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE IS 16384 BY DEFAULT and a DATA frame may not exceed
//      it. This bites at 16385 bytes — BEFORE flow control ever does — and the
//      client refuses the frame outright (`FrameTooLargeError`). So the first thing
//      a large response needs is not a window, it is being cut into frames.
//   3. THE FLOW-CONTROL WINDOW IS 65535 BYTES, per connection and per stream.
//      Past that the peer will not accept more DATA until it sends WINDOW_UPDATE.
//   4. INBOUND, THE SAME WINDOW IS OURS TO REFILL. A client sending a 70009-byte
//      request stalled at exactly 65535 with no WINDOW_UPDATE from us.
//
// THE ADVERTISED WINDOW IS DELIBERATELY LEFT AT THE DEFAULT. Sending a SETTINGS
// that raises SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE would make large requests work without
// any WINDOW_UPDATE logic — and would therefore make that logic untestable. The
// small window is what keeps the gate able to fail.
//
// FLOW CONTROL MAKES THE WRITER A READER. Before it, sending was a leaf: bytes went
// out and nothing came back. Now a send whose window is exhausted has to CONSUME
// INPUT — the WINDOW_UPDATE that reopens it — so the send path and the frame loop
// are mutually recursive. That is the one structural consequence of this whole
// section, and it is why `send_data` and `pump` are declared with `and`.
//
// BINARY CROSSES THE FFI AS BYTES, as of mere v0.1.281. `tcp_read` writes into the
// integer-addressed arena, and `mem_to_str` cannot bring that back — it stops at the
// first zero byte, which every second HPACK block contains.
//
// This file used to go arena -> hex -> `bytes` on the way in (two characters per
// byte) and call `mem_set_u8` ONCE PER BYTE on the way out. `mem_to_bytes` and
// `mem_copy_bytes` are the arena's two directions for data that may contain a zero,
// so both of those are gone. The per-byte write is the one that mattered: a response
// is now one call instead of one call per byte of it.

import "frame.mere";
import "hpack.mere";

// A handler answers with a message OR with a status. `bytes` alone could not say
// "this failed", so the server had no way to route an error and an unknown method
// got a reply that looked like a success. gRPC reports failure in the TRAILERS —
// the request succeeded as HTTP and failed as RPC — so this is not an HTTP status
// and cannot be expressed as one.
type grpc_reply =
  | RpcOk of bytes                 // one message, the unary case
  | RpcStream of bytes list        // many messages, server-streaming
  | RpcErr of (int * str);

// The peer's limits, and what is LEFT of the windows it has granted us.
//
// Threaded as a parameter rather than kept in a mutable cell, so a path that
// forgets to account for the bytes it sent cannot compile: `send_data` returns the
// new state and its caller has to use it.
//
//   cwin     the connection-level window (stream 0). Shared by every stream.
//   swin     the window for the stream being answered.
//   maxframe the peer's SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE — the largest DATA frame it will
//            accept. 16384 until it says otherwise, and it bites before cwin does.
//   initwin  the peer's SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE, remembered because a SETTINGS
//            arriving mid-connection ADJUSTS an open stream's window by the delta
//            rather than resetting it (RFC 9113 6.9.2).
type h2conn = { cwin: int, swin: int, maxframe: int, initwin: int };

extern fn tcp_listen: int -> int;
extern fn tcp_accept: int -> int;
extern fn tcp_read: int -> int -> int -> int;
extern fn tcp_write: int -> int -> int -> int;
extern fn tcp_close: int -> unit;
extern fn mem_alloc: int -> int;
extern fn mem_to_bytes: int -> int -> bytes;
extern fn mem_copy_bytes: int -> int -> bytes -> int;

module H2Server {

  let read_chunk = 16384;

  // --- status codes --------------------------------------------------------
  // The ones a server actually reaches for. Named because `12` at a call site is
  // a number and `H2Server.unimplemented` is a decision.
  let ok = 0;
  let cancelled = 1;
  let unknown_status = 2;
  let invalid_argument = 3;
  let deadline_exceeded = 4;
  let not_found = 5;
  let already_exists = 6;
  let permission_denied = 7;
  let resource_exhausted = 8;
  let failed_precondition = 9;
  let aborted = 10;
  let out_of_range = 11;
  let unimplemented = 12;
  let internal_status = 13;
  let unavailable = 14;
  let unauthenticated = 16;

  // --- grpc-message is percent-encoded -------------------------------------
  //
  // Not optional and not cosmetic: an HTTP/2 header value is bytes, but gRPC
  // specifies that `grpc-message` is percent-encoded, and a client DECODES it.
  // Measured — grpc-go turned `caf%C3%A9 %25 done` into `café % done` — so sending
  // a raw `%` would come back as the start of an escape and a raw UTF-8 byte would
  // be at the mercy of the client's decoder.
  //
  // Everything outside printable ASCII goes out as %XX, and `%` itself first.
  let hex_up = fn (n: int) -> if n < 10 then chr (48 + n) else chr (55 + n);

  let rec pct_at = fn (b: bytes, i: int, buf) ->
    if i >= bytes_len b then strbuf_to_str buf
    else
      let c = bytes_get b i in
      let _ =
        if c < 32 || c > 126 || c == 37 then
          let _ = strbuf_push buf "%" in
          let _ = strbuf_push buf (hex_up (bit_and (bit_shr c 4) 15)) in
          strbuf_push buf (hex_up (bit_and c 15))
        else strbuf_push buf (chr c) in
      pct_at b (i + 1) buf;

  let pct_encode = fn (s: str) -> pct_at (bytes_of_str s) 0 (strbuf_new ());

  let empty = bytes_of_hex "";

  // --- flow control constants ----------------------------------------------
  //
  // The defaults RFC 9113 fixes, named because a bare 65535 at a call site is a
  // number and `default_window` is the protocol saying something.
  let default_window = 65535;
  let default_max_frame = 16384;

  // The DATA payload one frame from here may carry. `wbuf` is allocated as this plus
  // a frame header, so this is a property of the buffer and not of the peer; the
  // peer's SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE is the other bound and the smaller wins.
  //
  // 65536 AND NOT 16384, so that BOTH bounds can decide the answer. With 16384 the
  // peer's value could never bind — SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE has a MINIMUM of 16384,
  // so a peer can only ever raise it, and `min(maxframe, write_cap)` was always
  // `write_cap`. Reading the setting was a term that could not change the result,
  // which is the same dead guard this directory already deleted once from
  // `frame.mere` — except here the fix is to make it live rather than to remove it:
  // a peer that allows 32768-byte frames gets half as many of them.
  let write_cap = 65536;

  // SETTINGS identifiers this server acts on. The rest are ignored, which is how
  // the protocol stays extensible — an unknown identifier is not an error.
  let settings_initial_window_size = 4;
  let settings_max_frame_size = 5;

  let new_conn = h2conn { cwin = default_window, swin = default_window,
                          maxframe = default_max_frame, initwin = default_window };

  // A SETTINGS frame from the peer. INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE adjusts the open stream's
  // window BY THE DELTA: a stream that has spent 1000 of a 65535 window has 64535
  // left, and a new size of 100000 must leave it 99000, not 100000. Resetting it
  // would hand back credit that was already spent.
  let rec apply_settings = fn (cn: h2conn, kvs) ->
    match kvs with
    | [] -> cn
    | [(k, v), ...rest] ->
      if k == settings_max_frame_size then
        apply_settings { cn | maxframe = v } rest
      else if k == settings_initial_window_size then
        apply_settings { cn | swin = cn.swin + (v - cn.initwin), initwin = v } rest
      else apply_settings cn rest;

  // A WINDOW_UPDATE's credit, applied to whichever window it names. Stream 0 is
  // the connection.
  //
  // AN INCREMENT OF 0 IS A PROTOCOL ERROR (RFC 9113 6.9) and it is refused HERE, in
  // one place, because it was previously decided in two: the frame loop silently
  // IGNORED a zero increment while the window-wait loop FAILED on it. Two spellings
  // of one rule, and only one of them was right — and the wrong one was on the path
  // every ordinary frame takes.
  //
  // Reporting it properly would need RST_STREAM or GOAWAY, neither of which this
  // server sends, so it fails and names the peer's bug. That beats waiting forever
  // for credit a conforming peer would have sent.
  let apply_window_update = fn (cn: h2conn, sid: int, inc: int) ->
    if inc == 0 then
      fail ("http2: a WINDOW_UPDATE increment of 0 is a protocol error (stream "
            ++ str_of_int sid ++ ")")
    else if sid == 0 then { cn | cwin = cn.cwin + inc }
    else { cn | swin = cn.swin + inc };

  // A new stream starts with the peer's INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE. The CONNECTION window
  // is NOT reset: it is per-connection, and a stream finishing returns no credit to
  // it. Resetting it here would invent credit the peer never granted, which shows up
  // as the peer refusing a frame rather than as anything happening at this line.
  let reset_stream = fn (cn: h2conn) -> { cn | swin = cn.initwin };

  // --- the unary case, as an adapter --------------------------------------
  //
  // The handler this module calls takes the request's messages as a LIST, because
  // that is what a client-streaming method sends. A unary handler should not have to
  // say so, so this wraps one.
  //
  // `[]` becomes an empty message rather than an error: proto3 gives a message no
  // way to distinguish "no bytes arrived" from "every field is at its default", so
  // for a unary method an absent request and an empty one ARE the same value. That
  // is the schema's rule, not a shortcut taken here.
  let unary = fn (h, path: str, msgs) ->
    match msgs with
    | [] -> h path empty
    | [m, ...rest] -> h path m;

  // One read. Returns the bytes that arrived, or empty when the peer is done.
  let recv = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int) ->
    let n = tcp_read cfd rbuf read_chunk in
    if n <= 0 then empty else mem_to_bytes rbuf n;

  // THE CAPACITY IS CHECKED HERE BECAUSE NOTHING BELOW CHECKS IT. `mem_copy_bytes`
  // is a memcpy into a bump-allocated arena that records no allocation sizes, so
  // there is no layer that could catch this: measured, 32 bytes into a 16-byte
  // allocation overwrote the next one and returned 32 as if it had worked.
  //
  // So this is the only bound that exists, and it fails loudly rather than
  // truncating — a short frame would be a protocol error the peer reports as
  // something else entirely, three layers from the cause.
  let send = fn (cfd: int, wbuf: int, b: bytes) ->
    if bytes_len b > write_cap + H2.header_len then
      fail ("http2: a " ++ str_of_int (bytes_len b)
            ++ "-byte frame does not fit the "
            ++ str_of_int (write_cap + H2.header_len)
            ++ "-byte write buffer — DATA must go through send_data")
    else
      let n = mem_copy_bytes wbuf 0 b in
      let _ = tcp_write cfd wbuf n in
      ();

  // Enough bytes for a frame header, and then for its payload. A frame can and
  // does arrive split across reads — the harness's own client sends the preface
  // and SETTINGS in one packet and HEADERS in another — so "read once and parse"
  // is not a simplification, it is a bug that happens to pass on a fast loopback.
  let rec ensure = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int, buf: bytes, want: int) ->
    if bytes_len buf >= want then buf
    else
      let more = recv cfd rbuf in
      if bytes_len more == 0 then buf                    // peer closed
      else ensure cfd rbuf (bytes_concat buf more) want;

  // `drop` is a reserved word (the language has `drop type`), so this cannot be
  // called what it is. Named for what it returns instead.
  let after = fn (b: bytes, n: int) -> bytes_slice b n (bytes_len b - n);

  // --- the reader inside the writer ---------------------------------------
  //
  // When a send exhausts its window, the only thing that can reopen it is a
  // WINDOW_UPDATE from the peer — so the writer has to consume input. This is the
  // one structural consequence of flow control: sending stops being a leaf.
  //
  // It returns the new state AND the unconsumed buffer, because frames arrive in
  // whatever grouping the network chose and the next one may already be in hand.
  // Dropping that tail would lose a whole request.
  //
  // What it accepts is deliberately narrow. A response is in flight, so a HEADERS
  // frame here is a second request interleaved with the first, which this server
  // does not serve. It FAILS AND NAMES THAT rather than skipping the frame: a
  // skipped HEADERS is a request that never gets an answer, and the client would
  // report a timeout with nothing in the log to say why.
  let rec await_window = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int, wbuf: int, cn: h2conn,
                             buf: bytes, stream: int) ->
    let b = ensure cfd rbuf buf H2.header_len in
    if bytes_len b < H2.header_len then
      fail "http2: the peer closed while DATA was waiting for a window"
    else
      let need = H2.header_len + H2.get_u24be b 0 in
      let b2 = ensure cfd rbuf b need in
      if bytes_len b2 < need then
        fail "http2: the peer closed mid-frame while DATA was waiting for a window"
      else
        let (_, ty, flags, sid, payload, nxt) = H2.read_frame b2 0 in
        let rest = after b2 nxt in
        if ty == H2.window_update then
          (apply_window_update cn sid (H2.window_increment payload), rest)
        else if ty == H2.settings then
          let cn2 = if H2.has_flag flags H2.ack then cn
                    else apply_settings cn (H2.read_settings payload) in
          let _ = if H2.has_flag flags H2.ack then ()
                  else send cfd wbuf (H2.frame_bytes H2.settings H2.ack 0 empty) in
          // RETURNS rather than recursing. A SETTINGS can RAISE the window — that
          // is the whole point of SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE — so a peer that
          // reopens a stalled stream that way and never sends a WINDOW_UPDATE is
          // conforming, and recursing here would wait for it forever.
          //
          // This line recursed for a while under a comment that said it did not.
          // Poisoning found it: a poison that RESET the window instead of adjusting
          // it by the delta went undetected, because the poisoned server was stuck
          // in this same loop and so looked exactly like the correct one.
          (cn2, rest)
        else if ty == H2.ping then
          let _ = if H2.has_flag flags H2.ack then ()
                  else send cfd wbuf (H2.frame_bytes H2.ping H2.ack 0 payload) in
          await_window cfd rbuf wbuf cn rest stream
        else if ty == H2.headers then
          fail ("http2: the peer opened stream " ++ str_of_int sid
                ++ " while stream " ++ str_of_int stream
                ++ " was still being answered — interleaved streams are not served")
        else if ty == H2.rst_stream then
          fail ("http2: the peer reset stream " ++ str_of_int sid
                ++ " mid-response — cancellation is not implemented")
        else await_window cfd rbuf wbuf cn rest stream;

  // --- one message, as as many DATA frames as the peer's limits require -----
  //
  // The chunk is the smallest of four bounds, and the order they bite in was
  // measured, not read off the RFC:
  //
  //   * the peer's SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE — 16384 by default, and it bites FIRST,
  //     at 16385 bytes, where the client refuses the frame outright
  //   * this buffer's capacity — the bound nothing below `send` enforces
  //   * the connection window and the stream window — 65535 each, biting at 65536
  //   * what is left of the message
  //
  // No END_STREAM anywhere: in gRPC the stream ends on the TRAILERS, so a DATA
  // frame here never carries it. A `last` parameter would be a flag no call site
  // ever sets, which is a claim the reader has to check for themselves.
  let rec send_data = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int, wbuf: int, cn: h2conn, buf: bytes,
                          stream: int, msg: bytes, off: int) ->
    let remaining = bytes_len msg - off in
    if remaining <= 0 then (cn, buf)
    else
      let win = if cn.cwin < cn.swin then cn.cwin else cn.swin in
      if win <= 0 then
        let (cn2, buf2) = await_window cfd rbuf wbuf cn buf stream in
        send_data cfd rbuf wbuf cn2 buf2 stream msg off
      else
        let cap1 = if cn.maxframe < write_cap then cn.maxframe else write_cap in
        let cap2 = if cap1 < win then cap1 else win in
        let n = if remaining < cap2 then remaining else cap2 in
        let _ = send cfd wbuf
                  (H2.frame_bytes H2.data 0 stream (bytes_slice msg off n)) in
        send_data cfd rbuf wbuf { cn | cwin = cn.cwin - n, swin = cn.swin - n }
                  buf stream msg (off + n);

  // --- the response ------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // HEADERS, then a DATA frame per gRPC message, then TRAILERS. The trailers are
  // not optional and not cosmetic — a gRPC client waits for `grpc-status` and
  // reports "unexpected EOF" without it, which is what makes this the only place a
  // missing frame looks like a network fault.
  let response_headers = fn (cfd: int, wbuf: int, stream: int) ->
    let h = Hpack.encode [(":status", "200"),
                          ("content-type", "application/grpc")] in
    send cfd wbuf (H2.frame_bytes H2.headers H2.end_headers stream h);

  let trailers = fn (cfd: int, wbuf: int, stream: int, kvs) ->
    send cfd wbuf (H2.frame_bytes H2.headers
                     (bit_or H2.end_headers H2.end_stream) stream (Hpack.encode kvs));

  // SERVER-STREAMING IS N DATA FRAMES AND ONE SET OF TRAILERS, not N responses.
  // Each message carries its own gRPC length prefix, so a client that reads the
  // stream sees message boundaries wherever the writer put them — independently of
  // where the HTTP/2 frame boundaries fell, which flow control moves around.
  let rec send_msgs = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int, wbuf: int, cn: h2conn, buf: bytes,
                          stream: int, msgs) ->
    match msgs with
    | [] -> (cn, buf)
    | [m, ...rest] ->
      let (cn2, buf2) =
        send_data cfd rbuf wbuf cn buf stream (H2.grpc_message m) 0 in
      send_msgs cfd rbuf wbuf cn2 buf2 stream rest;

  // An error is a status in the TRAILERS, not an HTTP status: the request
  // succeeded as HTTP and failed as RPC. There is no DATA frame — a failed call
  // carries no message — so this is HEADERS then trailers and nothing else.
  let respond = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int, wbuf: int, cn: h2conn, buf: bytes,
                    stream: int, r: grpc_reply) ->
    match r with
    | RpcOk msg ->
      let _ = response_headers cfd wbuf stream in
      let (cn2, buf2) = send_msgs cfd rbuf wbuf cn buf stream [msg] in
      let _ = trailers cfd wbuf stream [("grpc-status", "0")] in
      (cn2, buf2)
    | RpcStream msgs ->
      let _ = response_headers cfd wbuf stream in
      let (cn2, buf2) = send_msgs cfd rbuf wbuf cn buf stream msgs in
      let _ = trailers cfd wbuf stream [("grpc-status", "0")] in
      (cn2, buf2)
    | RpcErr (code, msg) ->
      let _ = response_headers cfd wbuf stream in
      let _ = trailers cfd wbuf stream [("grpc-status", str_of_int code),
                                        ("grpc-message", pct_encode msg)] in
      (cn, buf);

  // --- the request, split into gRPC messages ------------------------------
  //
  // A DATA payload is not a message: it is a stream of length-prefixed messages,
  // and the two boundaries are independent. CLIENT-STREAMING IS EXACTLY THIS LIST
  // HAVING MORE THAN ONE ELEMENT — there is no separate code path for it, which is
  // why it works without anything having been written for it.
  //
  // An empty buffer gives `[]`, which is a client that half-closed with nothing to
  // say. That is a different value from `[empty]`, and a unary method cannot tell
  // them apart — see `unary` below for why that is correct rather than convenient.
  let rec split_msgs = fn (d: bytes, i: int, acc) ->
    if i >= bytes_len d then list_rev acc
    else
      let (_, m, j) = H2.read_grpc_message d i in
      split_msgs d j (Cons (m, acc));

  // An empty `bytes list`, BUILT rather than written as `[]`.
  //
  // A bare `[]` at the call site below leaves its element type to be fixed by the
  // OTHER call site, and the C backend's monomorphiser then disagrees with the
  // typer about the handler's type: `mere -c` succeeded and the C it emitted said
  // `passing 'closure_str_closure_bytes_grpc_reply' to parameter of incompatible
  // type 'closure_str_closure_list_bytes_grpc_reply'`. Same family as Q-042 — an
  // empty collection literal whose element type is decided somewhere else.
  let no_msgs = split_msgs empty 0 [];

  // --- the frame loop ----------------------------------------------------
  //
  // `path` and `data` are the current stream's accumulation. `stream` is 0 until
  // a HEADERS frame names one. `cn` is the flow-control state, threaded so that a
  // branch which sends without accounting for it cannot typecheck.
  let rec pump = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int, wbuf: int, buf: bytes, st: hstate,
                     cn: h2conn, stream: int, path: str, data: bytes, h) ->
    let b = ensure cfd rbuf buf H2.header_len in
    if bytes_len b < H2.header_len then ()                // peer closed cleanly
    else
      let need = H2.header_len + H2.get_u24be b 0 in
      let b2 = ensure cfd rbuf b need in
      if bytes_len b2 < need then ()                      // truncated frame
      else
        let (_, ty, flags, sid, payload, nxt) = H2.read_frame b2 0 in
        let rest = after b2 nxt in
        if ty == H2.settings then
          // A SETTINGS that is not an ACK must be acknowledged, and the ACK
          // carries no payload. ITS VALUES ARE NO LONGER IGNORED: MAX_FRAME_SIZE
          // decides how large a DATA frame may be and INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE how much
          // may be in flight, so both are now things this server depends on.
          let cn2 = if H2.has_flag flags H2.ack then cn
                    else apply_settings cn (H2.read_settings payload) in
          let _ =
            if H2.has_flag flags H2.ack then ()
            else send cfd wbuf (H2.frame_bytes H2.settings H2.ack 0 empty) in
          pump cfd rbuf wbuf rest st cn2 stream path data h
        else if ty == H2.ping then
          let _ =
            if H2.has_flag flags H2.ack then ()
            else send cfd wbuf (H2.frame_bytes H2.ping H2.ack 0 payload) in
          pump cfd rbuf wbuf rest st cn stream path data h
        else if ty == H2.window_update then
          // Read and APPLIED. These used to be skipped, which was safe only while
          // no response was ever large enough to need the credit they carry.
          pump cfd rbuf wbuf rest st
               (apply_window_update cn sid (H2.window_increment payload))
               stream path data h
        else if ty == H2.goaway then ()
        else if ty == H2.headers then
          // The HPACK state is threaded, not rebuilt: the dynamic table is
          // connection state, and a second decoder would desynchronise on the
          // second request.
          let (hs, st2) = Hpack.decode st payload in
          let p = list_fold hs "" (fn (acc, kv) ->
                    let (n, v) = kv in if str_eq n ":path" then v else acc) in
          if H2.has_flag flags H2.end_stream then
            let (cn2, buf2) = respond cfd rbuf wbuf cn rest sid (h p no_msgs) in
            pump cfd rbuf wbuf buf2 st2 (reset_stream cn2) 0 "" empty h
          else pump cfd rbuf wbuf rest st2 cn sid p empty h
        else if ty == H2.data then
          let ln = bytes_len payload in
          let fin = H2.has_flag flags H2.end_stream in
          // INBOUND FLOW CONTROL. The window a client sends into is OURS to refill,
          // and nothing here refilled it: measured, a 70009-byte request stopped at
          // exactly 65535 bytes and waited forever.
          //
          // An increment of 0 is a protocol error, so an empty DATA frame — which is
          // how a client with nothing to say half-closes — gets no update at all.
          //
          // The CONNECTION window is refilled even on the frame that ends the
          // stream, because it is shared and permanent. The STREAM window is not,
          // because that stream will never carry data again.
          let _ = if ln == 0 then ()
                  else send cfd wbuf
                         (H2.frame_bytes H2.window_update 0 0 (H2.u32_payload ln)) in
          let _ = if ln == 0 || fin then ()
                  else send cfd wbuf
                         (H2.frame_bytes H2.window_update 0 sid (H2.u32_payload ln)) in
          let d = bytes_concat data payload in
          if fin then
            let (cn2, buf2) =
              respond cfd rbuf wbuf cn rest sid (h path (split_msgs d 0 [])) in
            pump cfd rbuf wbuf buf2 st (reset_stream cn2) 0 "" empty h
          else pump cfd rbuf wbuf rest st cn stream path d h
        else
          // PRIORITY, RST_STREAM, CONTINUATION: read and skipped. Skipping is only
          // correct because the extent is known from the header, which is the whole
          // reason a length-prefixed framing exists.
          pump cfd rbuf wbuf rest st cn stream path data h;

  let serve_conn = fn (cfd: int, rbuf: int, wbuf: int, h) ->
    let pre = bytes_of_str H2.preface in
    let b = ensure cfd rbuf empty H2.preface_len in
    if bytes_len b < H2.preface_len then tcp_close cfd
    else if hex_of_bytes (bytes_slice b 0 H2.preface_len) != hex_of_bytes pre then
      // Not HTTP/2. Closing is the whole answer: the preface is deliberately not
      // valid HTTP/1.1 so that this cannot be a half-understood request.
      tcp_close cfd
    else
      // OUR OWN SETTINGS ARE EMPTY ON PURPOSE. Raising
      // SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE here would let a large request through
      // without any WINDOW_UPDATE logic at all — and would therefore make that
      // logic untestable. The small default window is what keeps the gate able
      // to fail.
      let _ = send cfd wbuf (H2.frame_bytes H2.settings 0 0 empty) in
      let _ = pump cfd rbuf wbuf (after b H2.preface_len) Hpack.new_state
                   new_conn 0 "" empty h in
      tcp_close cfd;

  // `h` is `str -> bytes list -> grpc_reply`: a path and the request's messages,
  // giving one message, many, or a status. Everything about HTTP/2 — frames,
  // HPACK, windows — stays on this side of it.
  //
  // Wrap a one-message-in, one-reply-out handler with `H2Server.unary`.
  //
  // The parameter is called `h` and not `handler` for a reason that is now
  // HISTORY rather than a rule (Q-046, fixed in mere v0.1.283).
  //
  // A caller's own top-level `handler` used to collide with this parameter: the C
  // backend's inner-function lifting resolved the captured name to the CALLER'S
  // GLOBAL — the lifted loop took no handler parameter at all. It was invisible
  // while both had the same type, and surfaced only when `unary` made the types
  // differ. The compiler renames a colliding parameter now, so `handler` would be
  // a fine name here again.
  let serve = fn (port: int, h) ->
    let sfd = tcp_listen port in
    if sfd < 0 then fail ("http2: could not listen on port " ++ str_of_int port)
    else
      let rbuf = mem_alloc read_chunk in
      let wbuf = mem_alloc (write_cap + H2.header_len) in
      let rec loop = fn (n: int) ->
        let cfd = tcp_accept sfd in
        if cfd < 0 then ()
        else
          let _ = serve_conn cfd rbuf wbuf h in
          loop (n + 1) in
      loop 0;

  // --- a worker pool ------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // `serve` and `serve_n` take one connection at a time, which is not a throughput
  // question: a client that opens a connection and sends HALF a frame holds the server
  // for as long as it likes. `serve_pool` is the same accept loop with the connection
  // handed to one of a fixed set of workers.
  //
  // WHY A POOL AND NOT A THREAD PER CONNECTION. `mem_alloc` is a bump allocator with no
  // free, so a buffer pair per connection leaks a pair per connection. The arena decides
  // the shape: workers are fixed, each owns its buffers for the life of the process, and
  // the accept loop does nothing but accept.
  //
  // THE HPACK DYNAMIC TABLE IS PER CONNECTION AND STAYS THAT WAY. It lives in `pump`'s
  // arguments, so it lives on the worker's call stack — one worker handles one connection
  // at a time, which makes "per connection" and "per worker's stack" the same thing.
  // Nothing is shared between workers but the channel; two connections sharing a decoder
  // would desynchronise on the second request, which is the bug section 3 of
  // `grpc_parity` was written to catch in the single-connection case.
  //
  // SHUTDOWN IS `channel_close`, NOT A SENTINEL. `channel_recv_opt` returns `None` once
  // the channel is closed AND drained, which is the whole shutdown protocol: the accept
  // loop closes once, every worker finishes the connection it holds, and each sees
  // `None` when the queue is empty. A "negative fd means stop" token would work here
  // because a descriptor is never negative, but it makes the SENDER responsible for
  // knowing how many receivers there are, and it only exists as long as the channel
  // carries a type with a value to spare.
  let rec pool_worker = fn (ch, rbuf: int, wbuf: int, h) ->
    match channel_recv_opt ch with
    | None -> ()
    | Some cfd ->
      let _ = serve_conn cfd rbuf wbuf h in
      pool_worker ch rbuf wbuf h;

  // Returns the handles, because the parent has to WAIT for them: a worker still reading
  // a request keeps going after the accept loop is done, and a process that exits from
  // under it gives the client a connection reset instead of its answer.
  let rec start_pool = fn (ch, k: int, n: int, h, acc) ->
    if k >= n then acc
    else
      let rbuf = mem_alloc read_chunk in
      let wbuf = mem_alloc (write_cap + H2.header_len) in
      let t = spawn (fn (u) -> pool_worker ch rbuf wbuf h) in
      start_pool ch (k + 1) n h (Cons (t, acc));

  let serve_pool = fn (port: int, count: int, workers: int, h) ->
    let sfd = tcp_listen port in
    if sfd < 0 then fail ("http2: could not listen on port " ++ str_of_int port)
    else
      let ch = channel_new () in
      let handles = start_pool ch 0 workers h [] in
      let rec loop = fn (k: int) ->
        if count > 0 && k >= count then ()
        else
          let cfd = tcp_accept sfd in
          if cfd < 0 then ()
          else
            let _ = channel_send ch cfd in
            loop (k + 1) in
      let _ = loop 0 in
      // Close, THEN WAIT. Both halves are needed and the second is the one that is easy
      // to leave out: closing says "stop when you are free", and `join` is what makes
      // "free" happen before the process does. Without it a worker still reading a
      // request gets its client a connection reset instead of an answer.
      let _ = channel_close ch in
      // `fn (t) -> join t` and not `join`: a builtin cannot be passed as a value on any
      // compiled backend (Q-049). Same gap as `digit_p` in contrib/graphql/lexer.mere.
      list_iter handles (fn (t) -> join t);

  // Serving a bounded number of connections, so a harness can start the server,
  // make its requests and have the process exit on its own instead of being
  // killed. A test that has to kill its subject cannot tell a clean exit from a
  // hang.
  let serve_n = fn (port: int, count: int, h) ->
    let sfd = tcp_listen port in
    if sfd < 0 then fail ("http2: could not listen on port " ++ str_of_int port)
    else
      let rbuf = mem_alloc read_chunk in
      let wbuf = mem_alloc (write_cap + H2.header_len) in
      let rec loop = fn (k: int) ->
        if k >= count then ()
        else
          let cfd = tcp_accept sfd in
          if cfd < 0 then ()
          else
            let _ = serve_conn cfd rbuf wbuf h in
            loop (k + 1) in
      loop 0;
}
